-- DX EXPECTATION (not necessarily spec-required): a Unicode scalar
-- literal rejected for a SURROGATE or OUT-OF-RANGE code point should
-- say so, the way the same escapes already do in string context.
--
-- Spec 3.1.3 folds all four causes (zero scalars, several scalars,
-- surrogate, out-of-range) into one code, E_UNICODE_INVALID_SCALAR_
-- LITERAL, and 3.1.3's preamble frees tests from exact wording — so
-- the code is right and this is a message-quality pin. Today BOTH
-- '\u{D800}' and '\u{110000}' render the several-scalars message
-- ("must contain exactly one Unicode scalar value") plus a note
-- recommending a g'...' prefixed literal — but g'\u{D800}' is equally
-- invalid (surrogate escape, 6.3.1) and no g-literal can hold
-- U+110000, so the only guidance offered cannot fix either program.
-- In STRING context the compiler already distinguishes precisely:
-- "escape denotes a surrogate code point" / "escape denotes a code
-- point beyond U+10FFFF" (E_STRING_ESCAPE_INVALID). The scalar-literal
-- path should reuse those cause-specific renderings.
--
-- Expected: cause-specific wording per literal, no misleading g' note.
--! mode check
--! assertErrorCount 2
--! assertDiagnostic error E_UNICODE_INVALID_SCALAR_LITERAL
--! assertDiagnosticMatch surrogate
--! assertDiagnosticMatch beyond U\+10FFFF

badSurrogate : UnicodeScalar
let badSurrogate = '\u{D800}'

badRange : UnicodeScalar
let badRange = '\u{110000}'
-- STATUS(a0497523): FAIL — both literals render the generic "must contain exactly one Unicode scalar value" message with the misleading g'...' note; the cause-specific surrogate/out-of-range wording exists only on the string-literal path
